"""Explain every displayed formula without changing the fitted experiments."""
from common import ROOT
R=ROOT/'revision/review_v5'
p=R/'manuscript.md';text=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
def change(old,new):
 global text
 if new in text:return
 assert old in text,old[:90]
 text=text.replace(old,new,1)
change('Here Z has shape κ and scale θ, and δ is a nonnegative shift. Its uncensored mean μ and variance σ² depend on the ensemble mean and sample variance:',
 'In Eqn 1, Y is the nonnegative rainfall amount and Z is a latent gamma variable with positive shape κ and scale θ. The nonnegative shift δ creates a probability mass at zero: all values of Z at or below δ become dry forecasts. The gamma mean μ and variance σ², before shifting and censoring, depend on ensemble rainfall predictors:')
change('Six nonnegative parameters minimise closed-form CRPS, a distributional forecast loss in rainfall units.',
 'In Eqn 2, x̄ is the ensemble mean and s²ₓ is its sample variance across members. The six fitted parameters are a, b, c, d, e and δ; a and c provide baseline mean and variance, while b, d and e control their response to the ensemble. Positive μ and σ² give the gamma shape and scale through the last two identities. These are moments of Z, not of censored rainfall Y. Parameters minimise closed-form CRPS, a distributional forecast loss in rainfall units.')
change('The vector z contains the five standardised indices. H0 variance and shift remain fixed, so zero coefficients recover the base distribution.',
 'In Eqn 3, z contains standardised RMM1, RMM2, Niño 3.4, DMI and SAM values, and β contains their five fitted coefficients. The transpose makes βᵀz a scalar, dimensionless climate adjustment. Its exponential is positive, so the adjusted latent mean remains positive. H0 variance and shift remain fixed; β = 0 recovers the base distribution.')
change('The five-node matrix retains its first four rows and last row. Each additional aggregate has its own fitted marginal distribution.',
 'In Eqns 4 and 5, b is a 4 × 1 column of weekly rainfall amounts. The superscript T denotes transpose. The seven entries of y₇ are ordered as Week 1, Week 2, Week 3, Week 4, days 1–14, days 15–28 and days 1–28. The 7 × 4 matrix S₇ copies the weeks in its first four rows and sums them in its last three rows. S₅ retains the first four rows and the final row, giving a 5 × 4 matrix. All amounts are in millimetres, and both summing matrices are dimensionless. Each additional aggregate has its own fitted marginal distribution.')
change('Under unbiased base forecasts and the specified covariance, this linear mapping minimises reconciled error variance among linear unbiased mappings (Wickramasuriya et al. 2019).',
 'In Eqn 6, ŷ contains the separately calibrated forecasts, G maps them to four reconciled weeks, and S reconstructs every aggregate. For n = 5 or 7 nodes, S is n × 4, W is n × n and G is 4 × n. W is the covariance of observed-minus-predicted errors in mm²; the superscript −1 denotes matrix inverse. The identity GS = I₄ preserves an already coherent vector. Under unbiased base forecasts and the specified covariance, this mapping minimises reconciled error variance among linear unbiased mappings (Wickramasuriya et al. 2019).')
change('The four weekly amounts are the free variables. Enumerating their 16 active sets solves the weighted quadratic problem; aggregates are then calculated from the weeks.',
 'In Eqn 7, v is one joint sample from the calibrated distributions, and b* is the nonnegative weekly vector that minimises its covariance-weighted squared adjustment. The inequality applies to each week; argmin returns the minimising vector. The output v* is obtained by summing b*, which ensures that its aggregates agree. Enumerating the 16 possible sets of weeks fixed at zero solves this four-variable problem.')
change('Here s is the sum of the four weekly error variances. The adjustment depends on the discrepancy between the direct total and the sum of the weekly forecasts.',
 'In Eqn 8, m̂ is the direct 28-day forecast, ŵₖ is the base forecast for week k, and w̃ₖ is its reconciled value. The diagonal covariance contains the weekly error variances σ²ₖ and total error variance σ²ₘ, all in mm²; s is the sum of the four weekly variances. This identity describes unconstrained linear reconciliation with diagonal W. The bracketed quantity is the direct total minus the sum of the weekly forecasts, in millimetres. A week with greater error variance receives a larger share of that discrepancy.')
change('Lower CRPS is better. CRPSS is one minus mean CRPS divided by the score of permitted seasonal training climatology, evaluated on its full empirical support.',
 'In Eqn 9, y is the verifying rainfall amount and x⁽ⁱ⁾ is sample i at the same accumulation scale; M is the number of equally weighted samples. The first term is their mean absolute distance from the observation. The second uses all M² ordered sample pairs, including zero self-distances, and accounts for forecast spread. Together they give the CRPS of the empirical predictive distribution, in millimetres; lower is better. CRPSS is one minus mean CRPS divided by the score of permitted seasonal training climatology, evaluated on its full empirical support.')
change('This integrates CRPS loss above q. Further diagnostics include exceedance Brier skill relative to training probabilities, interval coverage and width, and randomised PIT histograms.',
 'In Eqn 10, max replaces each rainfall amount below q by q, for both forecasts and observations. This removes CRPS contributions below the threshold and retains the integrated loss above it, in millimetres. It scores every forecast case rather than only observed exceedances. Further diagnostics include exceedance Brier skill relative to training probabilities, interval coverage and width, and randomised probability integral transform (PIT) histograms.')
change('Finally, we test whether estimated error covariance explains seasonal mean-square-error gains. For the actual fitted linear projection P, a variance-based predictor of seasonal gain relative to bottom-up is',
 'Finally, we test whether training errors predict the benefit of combining scales. For each outer validation fold and season s, we estimate the proportional reduction in 28-day error variance relative to summing the four weeks:')
change('Ωₛ is the seasonal inner-error covariance and its bottom block contains weekly errors. We compare predicted gain with one minus the held-out MSE ratio, reporting bias, slope and Spearman association across 16 domain-season cases. Intervals resample common years. The prediction assumes negligible bias and stable error relationships, which are themselves evaluated.',
 'In Eqn 11, Vlin,s is the predicted error variance of the linearly reconciled 28-day total, and VBU,s is that of the weekly sum. Ωₛ is the 7 × 7 covariance of seasonal inner held-out mean errors, estimated with the same shrinkage rule as W. P is the fitted 7 × 7 linear projection. The subscript 77 selects the final diagonal entry, corresponding to the 28-day total. Ωₛ,bb is the upper-left 4 × 4 weekly block; 1₄ is a column of four ones. Its quadratic form sums all weekly variances and cross-covariances, giving the variance of their sum. Both V terms have units mm², so ĝₛ is dimensionless. A value of 0.10 predicts a 10% variance reduction; zero predicts no change, and a negative value predicts deterioration. Here s labels a season, independently of the variance sum used locally in Eqn 8.\n\nThe covariance identity follows because a coherent observation is unchanged by P: the reconciled error is P times the base error. Covariance therefore transforms as PΩₛPᵀ. We average the fold-specific predicted ratios and compare them with one minus the ratio of held-out mean squared errors across years. Mean squared error also includes squared bias. Agreement requires small bias and stable training-to-test error relationships; Eqn 11 does not predict CRPS improvement. It applies exactly to the fixed linear mapping, while the nonnegative sample projection is assessed separately as an empirical comparison. Bias, slope and Spearman association summarise the 16 domain-season cases; intervals resample common years.')
p.write_text(text,encoding='utf-8')
# Keep the unchanged older release intact while expanding supplementary definitions.
notes=(ROOT/'revision/review_v4/supplementary_notes.md').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
notes=notes.replace('[[EQ14]]','[[EQ14]]\n\nIn Eqn S1, T is the nonnegative 28-day total in millimetres, s₀ is a positive rainfall scale in millimetres, and a and b are fitted dimensionless coefficients. The ratio T/s₀ makes the power dimensionless. exp(a) changes magnitude and exp(b) is a positive exponent; a = b = 0 gives the identity map. Define g(0) = 0. These coefficients are separate from the EMOS coefficients in Eqn 2.')
notes=notes.replace('[[EQ15]]','[[EQ15]]\n\nIn Eqn S2, wₖ is a nonnegative weekly sample and T is the sum of its four weeks. When T > 0, each week receives its original proportion wₖ/T of the mapped total g(T). The proportions sum to one, so the four mapped weeks sum exactly to g(T). When T = 0, all four weeks are set to zero. This preserves temporal consistency but does not guarantee better probability scores.')
(R/'supplementary_notes_math.md').write_text(notes,encoding='utf-8')
p=R/'mixture_material.md';t=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
if 'In Eqn S3, j indexes' not in t:
 t=t.replace('Mixing assigns probability to whole trajectories and preserves their sums.', 'In Eqn S3, j indexes the three component distributions at origin t, wⱼ,ₜ is their nonnegative probability weight, and P*ₜ is the resulting joint probability distribution. The feature column xₜ includes an intercept; αⱼ is its coefficient column. These x features differ from the rainfall samples in Eqn 9. The denominator sums the three exponentiated scores, so their weights sum to one. Mixing assigns probability to whole trajectories and preserves their sums.')
 t=t.replace('The A terms are mean absolute distances from each component to the observation; the B terms are mean pairwise absolute distances between components, including within-component pairs. Equation (S4) applies to the marginal at each accumulation scale.', 'In Eqn S4, yₜ is observed rainfall at the chosen scale. Aⱼ,ₜ is the average absolute distance from component j samples to yₜ. Bⱼₖ,ₜ averages absolute distances over every pair formed from components j and k, using independent draws from their empirical distributions; within-component pairs are included when j = k. Both terms have rainfall units. The first sum measures distance from the observation and the double sum accounts for spread within and between components. Eqn S4 applies to the marginal at each accumulation scale.')
 p.write_text(t,encoding='utf-8')
print('Clarified all 11 main and 4 supplementary equations.')
